column361:
28/Feb/19 08:56;enesterovych;[~Jaanai], here:
{code:java}
CREATE TABLE my_schema.user ( user_name VARCHAR(255));
{code}

column362:
28/Feb/19 09:01;Jaanai;{quote}
SELECT * FROM USER LIKE USER_NAME = 'Some Name';
{quote}

This SQL can't be parsed by the newest version.

column363:
28/Feb/19 09:03;Jaanai;{quote}
CREATE TABLE my_schema.user ( user_name VARCHAR(255));
{quote}
There is also incorrect SQL grammar.

column370:
12/Apr/19 20:59;swaroopa;I ack! I was able to repro the problem. Working on it. 

I think like expects % operator in the quotes. 

Below two return same results. [~enesterovych]
SELECT * FROM my_schema.user WHERE USER_NAME LIKE '%Some Name';
SELECT * FROM my_schema.user WHERE USER_NAME = 'Some Name';
[~tdsilva] [~ckulkarni] Do you think if following queries should return the same results? Or should the user provide % in LIKE operator?
SELECT * FROM my_schema.user WHERE USER_NAME LIKE 'Some Name';
SELECT * FROM my_schema.user WHERE USER_NAME = 'Some Name';

column371:
12/Apr/19 22:44;ckulkarni;[~swaroopa] I think by PostgreSQL standard, 

SELECT * FROM my_schema.user WHERE USER_NAME LIKE 'Some Name';

should also work and since there are no "%" or "_" wildcards provided, it should return the same result as 

SELECT * FROM my_schema.user WHERE USER_NAME = 'Some Name';

