column51:
Reported on users@:
https://lists.apache.org/thread.html/063ad1651c559752080001c7faa40d24d0bf29d78636f3a3e222c1ab@%3Cusers.jena.apache.org%3E

Example:
{code}
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Location loc = Location.mem();
        DatasetGraph dsg = DatabaseMgr.connectDatasetGraph(loc);
        Txn.execute(dsg, ()->dsg.add(SSE.parseQuad("(_ :s :p '-1'^^xsd:float)")));
        Txn.execute(dsg, ()->RDFDataMgr.write(System.out, dsg, Lang.NQ));
    }
{code}

Package: org.apache.jena.ext.xerces.impl.dv;

Classes affected: SchemaDVFactory, DTDDVFactory.

For this self-contained use of Xerces code, we can just create the right class without class name, and newInstance.

Class JDOQLTypedQuery provides a method to create a numeric patameter for a query:

{{NumericExpression<?> numericParameter(String name);}}

This signature does not support the creation of a numeric parameter of type NumericExpression<Double> that is then used in methods like lt or gt taking a double field as an argument. 

I propose to add an overloaded method with the following signature:

{{<{color:#20999d}N{color}> NumericExpression<{color:#20999d}N{color}> numericParameter(Class<{color:#20999d}N{color}> type, String name);}}

I propose a corresponding extension for methods collectionParameter, mapParameter and listParameter:

{{<{color:#20999d}E{color}> CollectionExpression<Collection<{color:#20999d}E{color}>, {color:#20999d}E{color}> collectionParameter(Class<{color:#20999d}E{color}> elementType, String name);}}

{{<{color:#20999d}K{color},{color:#20999d}V{color}> MapExpression<Map<{color:#20999d}K{color},{color:#20999d}V{color}>, {color:#20999d}K{color}, {color:#20999d}V{color}> mapParameter(Class<{color:#20999d}K{color}> keyType, Class<{color:#20999d}V{color}> valueType, String name);}}

{{<{color:#20999d}E{color}> ListExpression<List<{color:#20999d}E{color}>, {color:#20999d}E{color}> listParameter(Class<{color:#20999d}E{color}> elementType, String name);}}

set storage group to root.ln.wf02.wt02;

create timeseries root.ln.wf02.wt02.status WITH DATATYPE=FLOAT, ENCODING=RLE, MAX_POINT_NUMBER=3

INSERT INTO root.ln.wf02.wt02(timestamp,status) values(5, 1.2345);

flush

select * from root
Time                                             | root.ln.wf02.wt02.status|
 1970-01-01T08:00:00.005+08:00|                               1.23|

++ 

1.23 should be 1.234 as expected

 

Problem is that in FileNodeManager.java:

 

  public void addTimeSeries(Path path, String dataType, String encoding) throws FileNodeManagerException {

   // this create a encoding converter and use right params

    FileNodeProcessor fileNodeProcessor = getProcessor(path.getFullPath(), true);

    try

{  

// this also create a encoding converter which override the converter above and use wrong params      fileNodeProcessor.addTimeSeries(path.getMeasurement(), dataType, encoding);     }

finally

{       fileNodeProcessor.writeUnlock();     }

  }

  FileNodeProcessor fileNodeProcessor = getProcessor(path.getFullPath(), true);

column360:
02/Mar/19 10:26;jixuan1989;I am trying to find a way to set the precision of data in memory. 

 

Using Float.valueOf(String.format("%.2f", data)) is ok but the performance is poor.

 

Then I have to use Math.round() to implement it. The performance is OK:
{code:java}
// code placeholder
public void testDouble(){
  double a;
  long start;
  start = System.currentTimeMillis();
  for (int i=0; i< 10000000; i++) {
    a=Math.random();
  }
  System.out.println((System.currentTimeMillis()-start));

  start = System.currentTimeMillis();
  for (int i=0; i< 10000000; i++) {
    a = Math.random();
    a = Math.round(a) + Math.round(((a - Math.round(a)) * Math.pow(10,10)))/Math.pow(10,10);
  }
  System.out.println((System.currentTimeMillis()-start));

  start = System.currentTimeMillis();
  for (int i=0; i< 10000000; i++) {
    a = Math.random();
    a =  Math.round((a * Math.pow(10,10)))/Math.pow(10,10);
  }
  System.out.println((System.currentTimeMillis()-start));
}

public void testFloat(){
  float a;
  long start;
  start = System.currentTimeMillis();
  for (int i=0; i< 10000000; i++) {
    a=(float)Math.random();
  }
  System.out.println((System.currentTimeMillis()-start));

  start = System.currentTimeMillis();
  for (int i=0; i< 10000000; i++) {
    a = (float)Math.random();
    if(a <Long.MAX_VALUE)
      a = Math.round(a) + Math.round(((a - Math.round(a)) * (float)Math.pow(10,10)))/(float)Math.pow(10,10);
  }
  System.out.println((System.currentTimeMillis()-start));

  start = System.currentTimeMillis();
  for (int i=0; i< 10000000; i++) {
    a = (float)Math.random();
    if(a <Long.MAX_VALUE)
      a =  Math.round((a * (float)Math.pow(10,10)))/(float)Math.pow(10,10);
  }
  System.out.println((System.currentTimeMillis()-start));
}
{code}
 

But, Math.round() only works for data which is less than Long.MAX_VALUE;

We may have to sacrifice this because performance is more important here.

