column85:
mcmellawatt commented on pull request #3322: GEODE-6488: Migrating cancellation state to execution context
URL: https://github.com/apache/geode/pull/3322#discussion_r267131917



 ##########
 File path: geode-core/src/integrationTest/java/org/apache/geode/cache/query/internal/QueryMonitorIntegrationTest.java
 ##########
 @@ -129,11 +134,52 @@ public void monitorQueryThreadCancelsLongRunningQueriesAndSetsExceptionAndThrows
     assertThat(queryExecutionCanceledException).isNotNull();
   }

+  @Test
+  public void monitorQueryExecutionThenStopMonitoringNoRemainingCancellationTasksRunning() {
+    cache = (InternalCache) new CacheFactory().set(LOCATORS, "").set(MCAST_PORT, "0").create();
+    final DefaultQuery query =
+        (DefaultQuery) cache.getQueryService().newQuery("SELECT DISTINCT * FROM /exampleRegion");
+    executionContext = new QueryExecutionContext(null, cache, query);
+    final ExecutionContext executionContext2 = new QueryExecutionContext(null, cache, query);
+
+    final ScheduledThreadPoolExecutor queryMonitorExecutor = new ScheduledThreadPoolExecutor(1);
+
+    final QueryMonitor queryMonitor = new QueryMonitor(
+        queryMonitorExecutor,
+        cache,
+        NEVER_EXPIRE_MILLIS);
+
+    final Thread firstClientQueryThread = new Thread(() -> {
+      queryMonitor.monitorQueryExecution(executionContext);
+
+      final Thread secondClientQueryThread = new Thread(() -> {
+        queryMonitor.monitorQueryExecution(executionContext2);
+        queryMonitor.stopMonitoringQueryExecution(executionContext2);
+      });
+
+      secondClientQueryThread.start();
+      try {
+        secondClientQueryThread.join();
+      } catch (final InterruptedException ex) {
+        Assert.fail("Unexpected exception while executing query. Details:\n"
+            + ExceptionUtils.getStackTrace(ex));
+      }
+

 Review comment:
   So the key here is that we called monitorQueryExecution for both threads before calling stopMonitoringQueryExecution on either thread.  If the bug wasn't fixed, we'd see that the cancellationTask reference from the first monitorQueryExecution call would be overwritten by the second monitorQueryExecution call (thereby losing the reference and leaking it to the executor).  So even after calling stop on both, the executor would still have the cancellation task from the first call/thread in its queue.  The other thing to note is that the expiration time is NEVER_EXPIRE_MILLIS, so timeout isn't coming into play at all here.

